          ":question/id": "b284307a-7bac-5101-a812-75d87d197409",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-8845",
          ":question/problem": "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+1)\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{vx}{x}\\right)+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =v\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v \\\\ \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo evaluate the integral on the left-hand side, we use integration by substitution with\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{u}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\operatorname{ln}\u2061v| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{C}|x|) \\\\ |\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\frac{y}{x}| & =|x|{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\frac{y}{x} & =\\pm {e}^{C}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\frac{y}{x} & =Kx \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & ={e}^{Kx} \\\\ y & =x{e}^{Kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced the new constant of integration:\n\n$$\nK=\\pm {e}^{C}\n$$"
