          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-90692",
          ":question/problem": "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{x}\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+2(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+2v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+2v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{{C}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{{C}_{1}}|x|) \\\\ |v+1| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}|x| \\\\ v+1 & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}x \\\\ v+1 & =Cx \\\\ v & =Cx-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =Cx-1 \\\\ y & =C{x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
