          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-14320",
          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+yx+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+yx+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}+(vx)x+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+v+1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={v}^{2}+v+1-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={v}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(v) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C) \\\\ y & =x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        }
      ],
