            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186088952,
              ":tutorial/content": "Given any homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation, we can always transform it into a separable equation through the substitution $y=vx,$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x.$\n\nTo understand why, suppose we have a homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y).\n$$\n\nThen because $f$ is homogeneous of degree zero, we have\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nfor any $t.$ By letting $t=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(tx,ty) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x,\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =F\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the function $F$ is defined as $F(v) = f(1,v).$ \n\nNow, letting $v = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ (or equivalently, $y=vx$), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =F\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(vx) & =F(v) \\\\ v+x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is now separable! We can separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v)-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{F(v)-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "fe0a9e9e-ab23-5364-99bb-cddbfd67a4a4",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 4107,
              ":tutorial/title": "Proof That Homogeneous First-Order Differential Equations Can Always Be Made Separable"
            },
            ":step/id": "e6e25f63-9efc-5f27-924c-23d2bc570eed",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 24848
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186088837,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186088871,
              ":tutorial/content": "Consider the homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{x+y}{x} \\,.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be solved using separation of variables. So, how can we solve it?\n\nGiven any homogeneous differential equation, if we substitute $y=vx$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x,$ then we can transform the differential equation into one that is separable.\n\nDifferentiating the substitution $y=vx,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the original differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential equation above is separable! We can solve it by integrating both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  substituting $y=v x$  we obtain the solution to the original differential equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =vx \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C)x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+Cx\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "d2f64582-8688-5882-baa3-8712370f1568",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2061,
